    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. so if the tenant tries to argue that the disruption was an \"unforeseeable chaotic event\" meant to imply something more than just a natural disaster—like something basically designed to mess with their operations—how does the burden of proof change? does the tenant have to prove the event was truly outside human control?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the burden of proof shifts heavily to the tenant to demonstrate that the event was truly unforeseeable and beyond all human control, effectively ruling out causation by malicious or planned sabotage. Under Singapore common law, once the claimant provides prima facie evidence, the onus moves to them to prove the strict elements of force majeure are met, including the total inability to perform. If the court finds the disruption stemmed from internal mismanagement, bad faith by a third party, or elements the tenant could have reasonably prevented, the force majeure defense will fail regardless of how chaotic the event appears.",
      "source": "target",
